#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124170 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0015e19e-222b-5c5b-a020-93392c50c10e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Best Linear Approximation of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/id #uuid "37734955-23b7-52d1-881d-0c77f672c35a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3162" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y, z)= xyz+y^2.$ Find the best linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3, -1, 1).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) \\approx f(a,b,c) + \\nabla f(a,b,c)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b, z-c \\rangle.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3, -1, 1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(3,-1,1)+∇f(3,-1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-3,y+1,z-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(3, -1, 1)= -2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =yz\\, &  & ⇒\\, &  & {f}_{x}(3,-1,1)=-1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =xz+2y\\, &  & ⇒\\, &  & {f}_{y}(3,-1,1)=1 \\\\ {f}_{z} & =xy\\, &  & ⇒\\, &  & {f}_{z}(3,-1,1)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(3, -1,1)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(3,-1,1) & =⟨-1,1,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(3,-1,1)+∇f(3,-1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-3,y+1,z-1⟩ \\\\  & =-2+⟨-1,1,-3⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y+1,z-1⟩ \\\\  & =-2-(x-3)+(y+1)-3(z-1) \\\\  & =-2-x+3+y+1-3z+3 \\\\  & =5-x+y-3z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124338 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "27e3c802-d356-5a2f-bdda-218144956722" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Two-Variable Function"}] :question/id #uuid "839eb8fd-39b8-5f1f-b833-7134d39cdf2d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8674" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,1) = 1, \\quad f_x(x,y) = 2xy, \\quad f_y(x,y) = x^2.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,1),$ estimate the value of $f(1.1, 0.9).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\approx f(a,b) + \\nabla f(a,b)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b \\rangle.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,1)+∇f(1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,1)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,1),{f}_{y}(1,1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩ \\\\  & =1+⟨{f}_{x}(1,1),{f}_{y}(1,1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find $f_x(1,1)$ and $f_y(1,1)$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =2(1)(1)=2 \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,1) & =(1)^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\approx 1 + \\langle 2, 1 \\rangle \\cdot \\langle x-1, y-1 \\rangle.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(1.1, 0.9)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x= 1.1$ and $y=0.9,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.1,0.9) & ≈1+⟨2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨1.1-1,0.9-1⟩ \\\\  & =1+⟨2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0.1,-0.1⟩ \\\\  & =1+0.2-0.1 \\\\  & =1.1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124340 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1639105-dca4-50d1-8d86-a7aed03ba0b5" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Three-Variable Function"}] :question/id #uuid "54ac5be6-5b6a-58a3-a960-0d9f18e7fc88" :question/math-academy-id "e-8692" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y, z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,2,5)=2, \\quad  f_x(1,2,5)=4, \\quad  f_y(1,2,5)=1, \\quad f_z(1,2,5)=-2.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,5)$ estimate the value of $f(1.01,2.02,4.95).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b, c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) \\approx f(a,b,c) + \\nabla f(a,b,c)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b,z-c \\rangle.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,5)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,2,5)+∇f(1,2,5)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-5⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,2,5)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,2,5),{f}_{y}(1,2,5),{f}_{z}(1,2,5)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-5⟩ \\\\  & =2+⟨4,1,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(1.01,2.02,4.95)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x= 1.01,$ $y=2.02,$ and $z=4.95$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.01,2.02,4.95) & ≈2+⟨4,1,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨1.01-1,2.02-2,4.95-5⟩ \\\\  & =2+⟨4,1,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,0.02,-0.05⟩ \\\\  & =2+0.04+0.02+0.1 \\\\  & =2.16\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335311 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "27e3c802-d356-5a2f-bdda-218144956722" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Two-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf475fe9-19b0-5dcf-ba6f-57d55775ec2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335313} {:answer/id #uuid "a205d2a5-0536-5cc9-838c-ee88f506562f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.85" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335314} {:answer/id #uuid "f701ad06-cc84-5692-a49a-6e14938f1ae7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.97" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335315} {:answer/id #uuid "c149e568-46a9-5748-8ae0-00e42601552d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335316} {:answer/id #uuid "b063c0bc-5392-5647-a46f-51b432aedca9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.74" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b063c0bc-5392-5647-a46f-51b432aedca9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.74" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335317} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2687be3-a511-55fe-9d21-779b0fd19a63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a5ffb4b6-2fa4-5a6f-a6dd-25f7f96b16a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-129634" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,-1)=-2,\\,{f}_{x}(1,-1)=1,\\,{f}_{y}(1,-1)=6.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1),$ estimate the value of $f(0.96,-0.95).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,-1)+∇f(1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,-1)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,-1),{f}_{y}(1,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩ \\\\  & =-2+⟨1,6⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(0.96,-0.95)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=0.96$ and $y=-0.95,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.96,-0.95) & ≈-2+⟨1,6⟩\\cdot ⟨0.96-1,-0.95+1⟩ \\\\  & =-2+⟨1,6⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.04,0.05⟩ \\\\  & =-2+1(-0.04)+6(0.05) \\\\  & =-2-0.04+0.3 \\\\  & =-1.74\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1639105-dca4-50d1-8d86-a7aed03ba0b5" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Three-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0670a411-5c45-573d-ac2c-a29cf26f6c8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335320} {:answer/id #uuid "30975257-6cd2-587b-ae1d-24e9a2b543cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.09" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335321} {:answer/id #uuid "663ab1c6-310d-5204-8090-ab68c1cd5285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335322} {:answer/id #uuid "8973736e-b698-5d4d-a77d-5e998b86e220" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.82" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335323} {:answer/id #uuid "eb18256a-7cdc-544e-8ee3-5316b2a50486" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0670a411-5c45-573d-ac2c-a29cf26f6c8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335320} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc124bfe-a409-5d29-b2e6-f65d217c76e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "726b98f5-3113-547b-995d-4663e2498149" :question/math-academy-id "q-129657" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,2,3)=6,\\,{f}_{x}(1,2,3)=6,\\,{f}_{y}(1,2,3)=3,\\,{f}_{z}(1,2,3)=2.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,3)$ estimate the value of $f(1.08,1.93,3.05)$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,2,3)+∇f(1,2,3)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-3⟩ \\\\  & =6+⟨{f}_{x}(1,2,3),{f}_{y}(1,2,3),{f}_{z}(1,2,3)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-3⟩ \\\\  & =6+⟨6,3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(1.08,1.93,3.05)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.08,$ $y=1.93,$ and $z=3.05$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.08,1.93,3.05) & ≈6+⟨6,3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨1.08-1,1.93-2,3.05-3⟩ \\\\  & =6+⟨6,3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0.08,-0.07,0.05⟩ \\\\  & =6+6(0.08)+3(-0.07)+2(0.05) \\\\  & =6+0.48-0.21+0.1 \\\\  & =6.37\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1639105-dca4-50d1-8d86-a7aed03ba0b5" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Three-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67885177-68dc-55cb-8839-4576cc313a2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6.01" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335369} {:answer/id #uuid "8b7841f5-cf7f-5712-9062-5317389e0236" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5.87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335370} {:answer/id #uuid "03007d32-556b-5252-b21a-ed92dbe88436" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5.98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335371} {:answer/id #uuid "b9453ff8-2af8-5651-af0f-7977ae7f73d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6.12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335372} {:answer/id #uuid "20bd1252-138f-5b02-bf93-781c065b83d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20bd1252-138f-5b02-bf93-781c065b83d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335373} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7311abe-11fe-5247-ad21-d4ddf394da3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46ef7f70-d725-51e8-9995-25f1cbb2c776" :question/math-academy-id "q-98348" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,2,-3)=-6, & \\,{f}_{x}(x,y,z)=yz,\\,{f}_{y}(x,y,z)=xz,\\,{f}_{z}(x,y,z)=xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,-3),$ estimate the value of $f(1.01,1.95,-3.02).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,-3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,2,-3)+∇f(1,2,-3)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z+3⟩ \\\\  & =-6+⟨{f}_{x}(1,2,-3),{f}_{y}(1,2,-3),{f}_{z}(1,2,-3)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z+3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(1,2,-3)$, ${f}_{y}(1,2,-3)$ and ${f}_{z}(1,2,-3),$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,2,-3) & =2(-3)=-6 \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,2,-3) & =1(-3)=-3 \\\\ {f}_{z}(1,2,-3) & =1(2)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈-6+⟨-6,-3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z+3⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(1.01,1.95,-3.02)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.01,y=1.95$ and $z=-3.02,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.01,1.95,-3.02) & ≈-6+⟨-6,-3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨1.01-1,1.95-2,-3.02+3⟩ \\\\  & =-6+⟨-6,-3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,-0.05,-0.02⟩ \\\\  & =-6-0.06+0.15-0.04 \\\\  & =-5.95\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335388 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0015e19e-222b-5c5b-a020-93392c50c10e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Best Linear Approximation of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3edb51c-8bc1-5e43-a79b-4891054f59cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈6-x-2y+z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335390} {:answer/id #uuid "1b35f050-d0ac-57fe-b923-292fbcac9a24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈8+x-2y-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335391} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ae3250-fa99-5810-ba7d-fc870a104f4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈6-x+y-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335392} {:answer/id #uuid "fae03db1-edc5-5270-8134-a60989997d41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈8-x+y+z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335393} {:answer/id #uuid "33064134-d89c-5a2d-9cf7-56b27083cd39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈6+x+2y-2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3edb51c-8bc1-5e43-a79b-4891054f59cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈6-x-2y+z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335390} :answer-field/id #uuid "a212bf02-5f2e-5905-860c-5dcbb5c5bde6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ec12843c-221e-5977-accb-d9e9aa80cb79" :question/math-academy-id "q-98555" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{9}{x+2y-z}.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(2,1,1)+∇f(2,1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-1,z-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(2,1,1)=3.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =-\\frac{9}{(x+2y-z)^{2}}\\,⇒\\,{f}_{x}(2,1,1)=-\\frac{9}{(2+2(1)-1)^{2}}=-1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =-\\frac{18}{(x+2y-z)^{2}}\\,⇒\\,{f}_{y}(2,1,1)=-\\frac{18}{(2+2(1)-1)^{2}}=-2, \\\\ {f}_{z} & =\\frac{9}{(x+2y-z)^{2}}\\,⇒\\,{f}_{z}(2,1,1)=\\frac{9}{(2+2(1)-1)^{2}}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(2,1,1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(2,1,1) & =⟨{f}_{x}(2,1,1),{f}_{y}(2,1,1),{f}_{z}(2,1,1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(2,1,1)+∇f(2,1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-1,z-1⟩ \\\\  & =3+⟨-1,-2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-1,z-1⟩ \\\\  & =3-1(x-2)-2(y-1)+1(z-1) \\\\  & =6-x-2y+z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
